{%- doc -%}
  Flattens a richtext setting to inline HTML: every block-level boundary becomes exactly one `<br>`,
  and the trailing one is dropped. Extracted from blocks/mionas-text.liquid so blocks/mionas-stamp.liquid
  can flatten the same way rather than restating the replace chain -- both render their result through
  snippets/mionas-text.liquid, whose `tag` allows `span` and h1-h6, elements that may only contain
  inline content. A raw `<p>` or `<li>` from the richtext toolbar inside an `<h1>` would be invalid
  HTML and get broken apart by the browser.

  Callers capture the output and gate on it, because an "empty" richtext value is `<p></p>` or
  `<p><br></p>` -- not blank -- and would otherwise emit an empty element:

    {%- capture content %}{% render 'mionas-inline-richtext', value: block.settings.text %}{% endcapture -%}
    {%- assign visible_text = content | strip_html | strip -%}

  Paragraph *spacing* is blocks/text.liquid's job (richtext rendered through `rte`), not this one's:
  a block rendering through here stays a single text node, matching Text.tsx's `children` (a ReactNode
  that may hold `<br/>`).

  @param {string} value - The richtext setting to flatten. Emitted unfiltered; nothing here escapes.

  @example
  {%- capture content %}{% render 'mionas-inline-richtext', value: block.settings.text %}{% endcapture -%}
{%- enddoc -%}
{%- liquid
  assign flattened = value | strip_newlines

  comment
    Closing tags never carry attributes, so a plain replace covers them. A list's own close adds no
    break because every `</li>` inside it already did.
  endcomment
  assign flattened = flattened | replace: '</p>', '<br>' | replace: '</li>', '<br>'
  assign flattened = flattened | replace: '</div>', '<br>'
  assign flattened = flattened | replace: '</h1>', '<br>' | replace: '</h2>', '<br>'
  assign flattened = flattened | replace: '</h3>', '<br>' | replace: '</h4>', '<br>'
  assign flattened = flattened | replace: '</h5>', '<br>' | replace: '</h6>', '<br>'
  assign flattened = flattened | replace: '</ul>', '' | replace: '</ol>', ''

  comment
    Opening tags may carry attributes, so they can't be dropped by matching '<p>' literally. A
    dynamic-source richtext value (e.g. product.description) can come back as
    `<p data-start="107">`, which survives a literal replace; nested inside the <p> that
    snippets/mionas-text.liquid emits, the browser auto-closes it and the copy loses every
    .text-role class.

    Liquid has no regex, so each opening tag is dropped by splitting on `<name` and stripping the
    following chunk's leading attributes up to its first `>`. The name must be followed by `>` or a
    space, or `<p` would also eat `<pre` and `<li` would eat `<link`.
  endcomment
  assign open_tags = 'p|li|ul|ol|div|h1|h2|h3|h4|h5|h6' | split: '|'
  for open_tag in open_tags
    assign marker = '<' | append: open_tag
    assign chunks = flattened | split: marker
    assign rebuilt = chunks | first
    for chunk in chunks offset: 1
      assign attributes = chunk | split: '>' | first
      assign first_char = attributes | slice: 0
      if attributes == blank or first_char == ' '
        assign tail = chunk | remove_first: attributes | remove_first: '>'
        assign rebuilt = rebuilt | append: tail
      else
        assign rebuilt = rebuilt | append: marker | append: chunk
      endif
    endfor
    assign flattened = rebuilt
  endfor

  comment
    Nested closes each contribute their own break, so `<div><p>Text</p></div>` becomes
    `Text<br><br>` and a single remove_last would leave a phantom blank line -- collapse runs
    first. `replace` is global, so each pass halves the longest run; six passes cover any nesting a
    richtext value can plausibly reach. A merchant's own deliberate double break is collapsed too,
    since the result must be one text node, not paragraph-spaced.
  endcomment
  for i in (1..6)
    if flattened contains '<br><br>'
      assign flattened = flattened | replace: '<br><br>', '<br>'
    endif
  endfor

  assign flattened = flattened | strip | remove_last: '<br>' | strip
-%}
{{- flattened -}}
